The dataset contains 9,742 movies with genre-based features, which were transformed into a 25-dimensional item matrix using TF-IDF and year scaling. The simulated user-item matrix (200 users × 9,742 movies) is highly sparse, reflecting real-world recommender system conditions where users rate only a small subset of items. From the results, Content-Based Filtering (CBF) produced highly similar recommendations (e.g., Toy Story → Toy Story 2, Antz, Monsters, Inc.), showing strong genre consistency but also indicating over-specialization. In contrast, Collaborative Filtering (CF) generated more diverse recommendations with moderate predicted ratings (~3.2), suggesting it captures broader user behavior patterns but may lack strong relevance to specific preferences. The Hybrid model successfully balances both approaches, producing recommendations that are both relevant and varied, with normalized scores improving ranking quality. The RMSE value of 1.24 indicates a reasonable prediction accuracy given the synthetic nature of the data, though it suggests there is still room for improvement with real user data and further tuning.
